{"id": "2026-11-17-news-ukrainian-strike-drops-crimean-bridge-span", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Ukrainian strike drops Crimean Bridge span", "summary": "A Ukrainian strike on 17 November collapsed the road span of the Crimean Bridge over the Kerch Strait, while Russian strikes closed two Kyiv bridges.", "url": "https://kyivindependent.com/ukrainian-forces-reportedly-strike-crimean-bridge-after-russian-missiles-hit-kyiv-bridges/", "source_domain": "kyivindependent.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Part of the Crimean Bridge across the Kerch Strait collapsed into the sea after a Ukrainian strike overnight into Tuesday 17 November, the second attack on the bridge in two days. President Volodymyr Zelensky said the strike was the \"seventh stage\" of an operation lasting several weeks. He said it was planned by the Security Service of Ukraine (SBU) and the General Staff, with Palianytsia drones hitting the road section and Neptune missiles, Ruta drones and FP-5 Flamingo missiles hitting the rail section. The SBU said one of the bridge's metal arches and the road span beneath it were \"completely destroyed\". Sergei Aksyonov, the governor Moscow installed in Crimea, said traffic was suspended and that a drone attack killed at least two people on the peninsula. On the same night Russia struck Kyiv's Northern and Southern bridges, which remain closed while engineers assess the damage. Mayor Vitali Klitschko said the metro was damaged and water, power and heating were partly cut on the Left Bank. The accounts of each side's strikes come from the two governments and could not be independently verified.", "why": "the 19 km bridge is Russia's only direct land link to occupied Crimea, which it annexed in 2014, and it carries supplies for the troops and some two million residents there. With the road span down, those supplies have to go by the land corridor through occupied southern Ukraine, which Ukrainian drones regularly attack. Both sides are now deliberately targeting each other's bridges.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-ukrainian-strike-drops-crimean-bridge-span", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Crimean Bridge", "Crimea", "Kyiv"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-five-eu-states-pick-rwanda-for-return-hub", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Five EU states pick Rwanda for return hub", "summary": "Germany, Austria, Denmark, Greece and the Netherlands announced with Rwanda a plan to transfer rejected asylum seekers to a return hub there from 2027.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/17/why-are-five-eu-countries-reviving-plans-to-deport-migrants-to-rwanda", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Germany, Austria, Denmark, Greece and the Netherlands announced with Rwanda in Vienna on Monday 16 November a plan to send rejected asylum seekers to a \"return hub\" in Rwanda, aiming to start transfers by 2027. The scheme would cover only people in the five countries who hold an enforceable removal order, including people from countries other than Rwanda. The five governments would pay the costs, including temporary accommodation. Their joint statement says people transferred could settle in Rwanda, go home voluntarily, or travel to another country they can legally enter. No formal agreement has been signed, and the governments have not said how many people they intend to send. Rights groups warn the arrangement could expose vulnerable people to abuse. In Germany the plan has already drawn criticism at home.", "why": "this is the first named destination for the \"return hubs\" the five agreed to pursue in principle in Copenhagen in September. It takes offshore deportation from a shared model to a named partner country, and Switzerland, as a member of Schengen and the Dublin asylum system, will have to decide where it stands on such schemes.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-five-eu-states-pick-rwanda-for-return-hub", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Rwanda", "Germany", "Austria", "Denmark", "Netherlands"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-mongolia-s-president-says-he-will-resign", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Mongolia's president says he will resign", "summary": "Mongolian President Khurelsukh Ukhnaa said he would resign over allegations he received $25 million linked to a coal railway contract.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/mongolian-president-khurelsukh-to-resign-amid-corruption-allegations/a-79964717", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Mongolia's President Khurelsukh Ukhnaa said on Tuesday 17 November that he will resign, after being accused of taking $25 million (about €23 million) linked to a state railway project. He said he was being \"falsely accused and defamed\" but would step down to \"restore my good name\", and pledged to cooperate with investigators. The resignation takes effect only once parliament, the State Great Khural, accepts it. The accusation came on Saturday 14 November from Nyambaatar Khishgee, a former mayor of Ulaanbaatar from a rival faction of the president's own Mongolian People's Party. He alleged bribes from Bodi International, which won the contract for the Tavantolgoi–Gashuunsukhait coal railway to the Chinese border without a competitive tender. Bodi denies paying any cash bribes. The opposition Democratic Party then called for Khurelsukh to go.", "why": "the railway carries Mongolia's coal exports to China, its main source of export revenue. The resignation adds to infighting in the ruling party, which has had three prime ministers in nine months.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-mongolia-s-president-says-he-will-resign", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Mongolia", "Khurelsukh Ukhnaa", "Bodi International"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-turkey-seizes-opposition-broadcaster-sozcu-tv", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Turkey seizes opposition broadcaster Sozcu TV", "summary": "Turkish police raided pro-opposition Sozcu TV and a trustee was installed in an anti-terror-financing investigation.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/turkish-authorities-raid-opposition-sozcu-news-channel-appoint-new-management/a-79966297", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Turkish police raided the Istanbul headquarters of the pro-opposition broadcaster Sozcu TV early on Tuesday 17 November, detained staff and installed a court-appointed trustee to run the channel and its news website. The Istanbul Chief Public Prosecutor's Office says the case concerns alleged breaches of anti-terror-financing laws and suspected links to the network of the late cleric Fethullah Gülen, whom Ankara blames for the 2016 coup attempt. Sozcu's lawyer says the raid stems from a 2016 case, and the channel has challenged the takeover in court. Regular news was replaced by a documentary for several hours before resuming at noon. The trustee did not take over the print newspaper.", "why": "appointing a trustee puts one of Turkey's last nationally broadcast critical news channels under state-chosen management. The prosecutor's \"terror financing\" label is the authorities' own characterisation, and it was used the same way in earlier takeovers of opposition media.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-turkey-seizes-opposition-broadcaster-sozcu-tv", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Turkey", "Sozcu TV"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-two-more-tankers-hit-in-hormuz", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Two more tankers hit in Hormuz", "summary": "UKMTO reported two tanker attacks in the Strait of Hormuz on 16 November while Iran's IRGC said tankers struck mines.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/17/tankers-hit-in-strait-of-hormuz-as-drone-raises-alarm-at-iraq-gas-field", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Two tankers were attacked in the Strait of Hormuz on Monday 16 November, the UK Maritime Trade Operations agency (UKMTO) reported, and Iran's Revolutionary Guards navy said two tankers had struck mines and caught fire. UKMTO said one tanker was disabled with an engine-room fire about seven nautical miles (13 km) north of Khasab, Oman. Another was hit by an unidentified projectile while leaving the strait. No casualties were reported. The Revolutionary Guards said their two tankers were using a route Tehran considers unauthorised, and Iran's regular navy reported a third vessel hitting a mine east of the strait. Iran named none of the ships, and it is unclear whether its claims and the UKMTO incidents describe the same events. The UN's International Maritime Organization counted 136 security incidents involving vessels in the region between 1 March and 11 November, 35 of them in October.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-two-more-tankers-hit-in-hormuz", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Strait of Hormuz", "Iran", "UKMTO"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-deutsche-bank-leak-exposes-swiss-oligarch-accoun", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Deutsche Bank leak exposes Swiss oligarch accounts", "summary": "A leak of 15 million Deutsche Bank documents shows the bank held about twenty Putin-linked oligarchs' fortunes via Zurich and Geneva.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/economie/finance/fortunes-d-oligarques-russes-heberges-en-suisse-relations-avec-jeffrey-epstein-une-fuite-massive-de-documents-secoue-deutsche-bank", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A leak of more than 15 million internal Deutsche Bank documents, published on Tuesday 17 November by a consortium of over 200 journalists, shows the German bank held the fortunes of about twenty Russian oligarchs close to Vladimir Putin through its Zurich and Geneva units before the 2022 invasion of Ukraine. The consortium includes the Tamedia papers in Switzerland, Der Spiegel, the Financial Times and Le Monde. According to the reporting, the bank rated those clients by colour for risk. The highest-risk \"red\" category included Suleyman Kerimov and Viktor Vekselberg, who lived in Zug. The documents also show a late-2020 plan, drawn up by a working group that included chief executive Christian Sewing, to grow revenue from wealthy Russian clients despite internal and external warnings about money-laundering risk. Deutsche Bank says some of the facts are nearly twenty years old, that regulators reviewed them at the time, and that it spent €7 billion on controls between 2019 and 2025. Its shares fell almost 6% in early Frankfurt trading.", "why": "the leak puts the Swiss financial centre back at the heart of how Russian elite wealth was held. It also raises the question of what Swiss supervisors knew about these Zurich and Geneva accounts at the time.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-deutsche-bank-leak-exposes-swiss-oligarch-accoun", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Deutsche Bank", "Suleyman Kerimov", "Viktor Vekselberg", "Zurich", "Geneva"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-drought-cuts-swiss-farm-value-added-11-7", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Drought cuts Swiss farm value added 11.7%", "summary": "FSO first estimates show Swiss agricultural gross value added falling 11.7% in 2026 after a historic drought.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/MMadk29ey8pJ", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Swiss farming's gross value added will fall 11.7% in 2026 to 4.5 billion francs, according to first estimates from the Federal Statistical Office (FSO) published on Tuesday 17 November, after a historic summer heatwave and drought. Total production value is expected to fall 5.1% to 12.0 billion francs. Crop production value is down 9.0%. Fodder is down 19.2% after poor yields forced farmers to use up their reserves early. Sugar beet (−33%) and potatoes (−24.8%) are heading for their lowest harvests in 30 years. Pig farmers were hit separately: output rose 5.3% while prices fell 34.3%. Beef did well, with production value up 10.6% to a level last seen in the early 1990s. Without the effect of prices, value added fell only 2.2%. Labour productivity is down 0.7% but still 38% above its 2000 level.", "why": "the FSO compares 2026 against an \"excellent\" 2025, which exaggerates the fall in francs. The record lows for sugar beet and potatoes are a clearer measure of the drought, and they will weigh on the farm-support debates ahead of next year's budget.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-drought-cuts-swiss-farm-value-added-11-7", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Statistical Office", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-iv-offices-may-flag-gun-risks", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "IV offices may flag gun risks", "summary": "The Federal Council recommends accepting a motion letting IV offices report insured people unfit to hold weapons to cantonal weapons offices.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/nach-amoktat-von-aarau-iv-soll-gefaehrliche-personen-der-waffenbehoerde-melden-duerfen", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council has recommended that parliament accept a motion allowing cantonal disability-insurance (IV) offices to report insured people they consider unfit to own a gun to cantonal weapons offices, SRF reported on Tuesday 17 November. The motion was filed by Jura SP National Councillor Loïc Dobler after the shooting in Aarau at the end of August. In that attack a 43-year-old man from Jura who legally owned weapons killed a young woman and wounded six people, and according to his parents he drew an IV pension for psychiatric problems. IV staff can already file reports in a personal capacity, like any official. Dobler, who works at the Jura IV office, says almost nobody does so because they carry the risk personally, and that letting the office report as an authority would change that. The Federal Council took its decision on Wednesday 11 November. The Federal Social Insurance Office declined to explain its reasons.", "why": "the change would create a formal link between social-insurance health information and gun licensing. That closes a gap the Aarau case exposed, but it also raises questions about medical confidentiality and stigma for IV recipients with mental illness, which parliament will have to weigh.", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-iv-offices-may-flag-gun-risks", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-11", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "Loïc Dobler", "Aarau"]}
{"id": "2026-11-17-news-swiss-housing-costs-rise-as-incomes-stall", "date": "2026-11-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss housing costs rise as incomes stall", "summary": "FSO household budget survey: 2024 disposable income flat at 7,209 francs a month while housing spending rose to 1,546 francs.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/6f7-asx4KxXe", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Swiss households had an average disposable income of 7,209 francs a month in 2024, almost unchanged from 2023 (7,186), while housing costs kept rising to 1,546 francs a month, the FSO's household budget survey published on Tuesday 17 November shows. Housing is the largest consumer spending item for Swiss households. Compulsory spending (taxes, social contributions, health-insurance premiums and transfers) took 31.0% of gross income, with taxes alone at 12.2% and premiums at 7.2%. About 61% of households have a disposable income below the average, which is pulled up by high earners. Over 2022–2024 the gap ran from 10,569 francs for couples with children to 3,591 francs for single people aged 65 or over.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-17-news-swiss-housing-costs-rise-as-incomes-stall", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-17", "event_date": "2026-11-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Statistical Office", "Switzerland"]}
